# water_absorption.spectra
#
# Pure water spectral absorption, 380-700 nm, source: Pope, R.M. and Fry, E.S.
# (1997) "Absorption Spectrum (380-700 nm) of Pure Water. II. Integrating
# Cavity Measurements", Applied Optics 36(33):8710-8723.  Values below are the
# published absorption coefficient a(lambda) in m^-1 (the units of the paper's
# Table 3 as conventionally cited), resampled onto a 10 nm grid from the
# source's native 2.5 nm spacing (docs/WETNESS_COAT_DESIGN.md section 6.7
# cites the anchors a(450)~=0.0092 m^-1, a(650)~=0.34 m^-1, a(700)~=0.624 m^-1
# as the go/no-go check on this conversion; all three match the source table
# exactly).  Minimum absorption is ~0.0044 m^-1 near 418 nm (the well-known
# Pope & Fry blue-absorption result); absorption rises steeply past 590 nm as
# the O-H stretch overtone band is approached.
#
# CONVERSION -- this file is a TRANSMITTANCE BASE, not an absorption
# coefficient.  dielectric_material's `tau` is applied as pow(tau, distance)
# (DielectricSPF.cpp:311-324), i.e. Beer-Lambert with tau as the per-unit-
# distance base, NOT as -sigma_a directly.  Each value below is therefore
# exp(-a(lambda)) with a(lambda) in m^-1, so that pow(tau, distance_in_metres)
# reproduces exp(-a(lambda) * distance_in_metres) exactly.  Pasting a raw
# published absorption coefficient straight into `tau` -- skipping this
# exp(-a) step -- inverts the curve: blue would come out heavily attenuated
# and red would pass freely, backwards from real water.
#
# WORLD-SCALE ASSUMPTION -- "per unit distance" bakes in 1 WORLD UNIT = 1
# METRE.  A scene built at a different scale must rescale every value:
# tau_new = tau_old ^ (1 / s), where s is world units per metre (e.g. a
# centimetre-scaled scene has s = 0.01, so tau_new = tau_old ^ 100 -- a much
# stronger per-unit-distance attenuation, matching the fact that a "1 unit"
# path is now only 1 cm of real water).
#
# USAGE (spectral rasterizers) -- `file` resolves through GlobalMediaPathLocator,
# same as every other colors/*.spectra reference in the tree (e.g.
# scenes/Tests/VCM/cornellbox_vcm_spectral.RISEscene's `file colors/cornellbox_green.spectra`),
# so the `colors/` prefix is required, unquoted:
#   scalar_painter { name water_tau  file colors/water_absorption.spectra }
#   dielectric_material { name deep_water  tau water_tau  ior 1.333  scattering 1000000 }
#
# RGB IDIOM (debt 10, PiecewiseLinearScalarPainter::GetValuesAt broadcasts a
# single 555 nm sample under an RGB rasterizer -- see the header note on
# HasPerChannelVariation() -- so the file above is INERT under the default
# pathtracing_pel_rasterizer: it renders grey water with no depth tint at
# all).  For an RGB rasterizer, author three explicit per-channel numbers
# instead, sampling this same table at representative R/G/B wavelengths
# (610 / 550 / 465 nm below) -- the same idiom already shipped at
# scenes/FeatureBased/Combined/tidepools.RISEscene:395 (`tau 0.85 0.92 0.95`,
# an art-directed shallow-pool value, not this file's 1-metre physical base):
#   dielectric_material { name deep_water_rgb  tau 0.77 0.95 0.99  ior 1.333  scattering 1000000 }
# Rescale per the WORLD-SCALE note above if the scene is not metre-scaled.
#
380	0.988694
390	0.991526
400	0.993392
410	0.995281
420	0.995470
430	0.995062
440	0.993670
450	0.990822
460	0.990258
470	0.989456
480	0.987380
490	0.985112
500	0.979807
510	0.968022
520	0.959925
530	0.957528
540	0.953706
550	0.945066
560	0.939977
570	0.932860
580	0.914297
590	0.873629
600	0.800595
610	0.767666
620	0.759192
630	0.747067
640	0.732860
650	0.711770
660	0.663650
670	0.644681
680	0.628135
690	0.596903
700	0.535797
